	An IRunnableContext supports to run a runnable forked:
		public void run(boolean fork, boolean cancelable, IRunnableWithProgress runnable) throws InvocationTargetException, InterruptedException {
	When run in forked mode the runnable is executed in a separate ModalContext thread and the UI is blocked from input.
	When forked is false then the operation is run in the UI thread. This means the UI isn't responsive while the runnable is
	executing. 

	Since the JFace restructuring most of the code was run with fork=false and this resulted in PRs since the UI wasn't repainting. 
	This got addressed by turning the fork to true. However, this is no longer trivial since the code now has to
	be aware that it isn't running in the UI thread. Notice, that in the old JFace there were some smarts in dialogs that
	took some of this burden from the user. 

	We have attempted to switch to fork=true in the Java UI. Posting dialogs from another thread is still doable with
	a code pattern like this:

	private int openDialog(final Dialog dialog) {
		final int[] result= { Dialog.CANCEL };
		getViewer().getControl().getDisplay().syncExec(new Runnable() {
			public void run() {
				result[0]= dialog.open();
			}
		});
		return result[0];
	}		 

	However, where we hit the wall was when a long running operation tries to modify a Document.
	JFace text is consistent with JFace and assumes it is accessed in the UI thread only.

	Issues:
	- 	there should be a huge WARNING in IRunnableContext about the consequences of 
		using forked=true
	- 	there needs to be recommendations for how ISVs handle forked=true, e.g. how to
		post dialogs.
	- 	the Document modification problem could be addressed by making the Document
		aware that it can be accessed from a non-UI thread. However, this would be 
		inconsistent with the rest of JFace.
	-	without a better story for forked=true clients will fall back to forked=false which
		results in non-responsive UI behaviour.


